/* 200 US cities with 3 checked fun facts each. inGame:true = in net.mjs. */
export const FACTS={
'Portland ME':{state:'Maine',lon:-70.26,lat:43.66,inGame:true,facts:['Canned corn was developed in Portland by the N. Winslow company.','Since 1976, a mystery “Valentine’s Day Phantom” has covered Portland in hearts.','Portland’s Amato’s is reported to be the birthplace of the Maine Italian sandwich.']},
'Boston':{state:'Massachusetts',lon:-71.06,lat:42.36,inGame:true,facts:['More than two-thirds of inner Boston’s land didn’t exist when the city began.','Back Bay was filled in with gravel brought by train from Needham.','Boston baked beans came from the city’s heavy trade in Caribbean molasses.']},
'Burlington':{state:'Vermont',lon:-73.21,lat:44.48,inGame:true,facts:['Ben & Jerry’s opened its first scoop shop in an old gas station in Burlington.','Burlington’s electricity became entirely renewable in 2015.','Burlington launched the world’s first lake-going steamboat in 1808.']},
'Hartford':{state:'Connecticut',lon:-72.68,lat:41.76,inGame:true,facts:['The Hartford Courant has been published continuously since 1764.','Royal and Underwood made Hartford the Typewriter Capitol of the World.','Mark Twain called Hartford the chief of all the beautiful towns he had seen.']},
'Albany':{state:'New York',lon:-73.76,lat:42.65,inGame:true,facts:['Albany was the original eastern end of the Erie Canal.','Albany is named for a Scottish duke, and Alba is the Gaelic name for Scotland.','Albany’s spring Tulip Festival celebrates the city’s Dutch roots.']},
'New York':{state:'New York',lon:-74.01,lat:40.71,inGame:true,facts:['An estimated 800 languages are spoken in New York City.','New York City became the nation’s capital in 1785.','New York’s Central Park was the first landscaped park in an American city.']},
'Syracuse':{state:'New York',lon:-76.15,lat:43.05,inGame:true,facts:['Syracuse has a traffic light with green on top, put up after kids kept hitting red.','Syracuse sits about halfway between New York City and Toronto.','Syracuse is named for a Sicilian city with a lake and salt springs like its own.']},
'Buffalo':{state:'New York',lon:-78.88,lat:42.89,inGame:true,facts:['Teressa Bellissimo first made Buffalo wings at the Anchor Bar here in 1964.','The Electric Tower at Buffalo’s 1901 world’s fair had over two million light bulbs.','Buffalo’s Joseph Dart helped create the grain elevator in 1843.']},
'Scranton':{state:'Pennsylvania',lon:-75.66,lat:41.41,inGame:true,facts:['Scranton earned the nickname The Electric City from its electric trolley system.','Scranton was once the capital of the anthracite coal industry.','The Office was set at a fictional paper company’s Scranton branch.']},
'Philadelphia':{state:'Pennsylvania',lon:-75.17,lat:39.95,inGame:true,facts:['Elfreth’s Alley in Philadelphia has had homes on it since 1703.','The Liberty Bell was hidden in Allentown for about nine months in 1777.','Philadelphia has more outdoor sculptures and murals than any other US city.']},
'Harrisburg':{state:'Pennsylvania',lon:-76.88,lat:40.26,inGame:true,facts:['The Pennsylvania Farm Show began in Harrisburg in 1917 and is held there yearly.','Harrisburg’s Capitol dome was modeled on St. Peter’s Basilica in Rome.','At its 1904 peak, the Pennsylvania Railroad made 100 passenger stops a day in Harrisburg.']},
'Pittsburgh':{state:'Pennsylvania',lon:-80.00,lat:40.44,inGame:true,facts:['Pittsburgh is called the City of Bridges for its 446 bridges.','Pittsburgh TV gave the nation Mister Rogers’ Neighborhood.','Pittsburgh claims to have the world’s first movie theater.']},
'Erie':{state:'Pennsylvania',lon:-80.09,lat:42.13,inGame:true,facts:['Erie is named for the Erie people, who lived here until the mid-1600s.','Presque Isle, a sandy peninsula, juts out into Lake Erie right beside Erie.','Erie once set Pennsylvania’s record for the largest two-day snowfall.']},
'Baltimore':{state:'Maryland',lon:-76.61,lat:39.29,inGame:true,facts:['John Waters called Baltimore’s formstone “the polyester of brick.”','The world’s first dental college opened in Baltimore in 1840.','Baltimore’s National Brewing Company introduced the first six-pack in the 1940s.']},
'Washington':{state:'District of Columbia',lon:-77.04,lat:38.90,inGame:true,facts:['Georgetown’s Old Stone House was built in 1765 and still stands in D.C.','Surveyors set a stone at each mile of D.C.’s border, and many still stand.','Rock Creek Park runs about 9 miles through the middle of Washington.']},
'Richmond':{state:'Virginia',lon:-77.44,lat:37.54,inGame:true,facts:['Richmond’s oldest stone house is now a museum about Edgar Allan Poe.','Richmond sits at the James River’s fall line, where the river drops over rapids.','Patrick Henry gave his “liberty or death” speech at a Richmond church in 1775.']},
'Norfolk':{state:'Virginia',lon:-76.29,lat:36.85,inGame:true,facts:['Naval Station Norfolk is called the world’s largest naval installation.','You can tour the battleship USS Wisconsin at the Nauticus center in Norfolk.','Norfolk’s Mermaids on Parade program put mermaid statues all over the city.']},
'Raleigh':{state:'North Carolina',lon:-78.64,lat:35.78,inGame:true,facts:['Raleigh’s street grid was modeled on Philadelphia’s 1682 city plan.','President Andrew Johnson was born at Casso’s Inn in Raleigh.','Raleigh is known as the City of Oaks for its oak-lined streets.']},
'Charlotte':{state:'North Carolina',lon:-80.84,lat:35.23,inGame:true,facts:['Charlotte is named after a queen, Charlotte of Mecklenburg-Strelitz.','Charlotte’s old U.S. Mint building now houses an art museum.','Charlotte earned the nickname the City of Churches for its many 19th-century churches.']},
'Charleston':{state:'South Carolina',lon:-79.93,lat:32.78,inGame:true,facts:['Dock workers’ geechee dances in Charleston inspired Eubie Blake’s Charleston Rag.','Charleston’s Holy City nickname was probably coined in the 1900s as a mockery.','The Charleston Museum, founded in 1773, is called America’s first museum.']},
'Columbia':{state:'South Carolina',lon:-81.03,lat:34.00,inGame:true,facts:['Columbia was planned as a town of 400 blocks in a two-mile square.','The Saluda and Broad rivers merge in Columbia to form the Congaree River.','Columbia is home to Fort Jackson, the Army’s largest Basic Combat Training base.']},
'Savannah':{state:'Georgia',lon:-81.10,lat:32.08,inGame:true,facts:['The Forrest Gump bench scene was filmed on Chippewa Square in Savannah.','Girl Scouts founder Juliette Gordon Low was born in Savannah.','Savannah’s live oaks earned it an old nickname, the Forest City.']},
'Atlanta':{state:'Georgia',lon:-84.39,lat:33.75,inGame:true,facts:['Atlanta was once called Terminus, then Marthasville after a governor’s daughter.','More than 55 streets in Atlanta have Peachtree in their names.','Georgia Tech, based in Atlanta, once beat Cumberland College 222 to 0 in football.']},
'Jacksonville':{state:'Florida',lon:-81.66,lat:30.33,inGame:true,facts:['Jacksonville is the biggest city in the lower 48 by total area.','More than 30 silent film studios opened in Jacksonville in the 1910s.','An early name for Jacksonville’s river crossing was Cow Ford.']},
'Orlando':{state:'Florida',lon:-81.38,lat:28.54,inGame:true,facts:['Orlando was once known as Jernigan.','One story says Orlando was named after a Shakespeare play, As You Like It.','The Orlando area is dotted with hundreds of lakes.']},
'Tampa':{state:'Florida',lon:-82.46,lat:27.95,inGame:true,facts:['Tampa’s old Plant hotel had six minarets and is now part of the University of Tampa.','Tampa’s Cuban sandwich traditionally has Genoa salami, likely from Italian immigrants.','Each winter Tampa stages Gasparilla, a mock pirate invasion held since 1904.']},
'Miami':{state:'Florida',lon:-80.19,lat:25.76,inGame:true,facts:['Miami was built on land owned by citrus grower Julia Tuttle.','A hard freeze in 1894 spared Miami’s crops and helped the young city grow.','Alligators from the Everglades sometimes wander into Miami neighborhoods.']},
'Tallahassee':{state:'Florida',lon:-84.28,lat:30.44,inGame:true,facts:['Tallahassee’s name is a Muskogean word often translated as “old fields” or “old town.”','Spanish explorers camped here in 1539, often cited as America’s first Christmas.','Meridian Road here follows a line surveyed in 1824 to map all of Florida.']},
'Birmingham':{state:'Alabama',lon:-86.80,lat:33.52,inGame:true,facts:['Birmingham sits where iron ore, coal and limestone all lie close together.','Birmingham’s giant Vulcan statue was first cast for the 1904 St. Louis fair.','Birmingham’s Barber museum holds one of the biggest motorcycle collections on Earth.']},
'Mobile':{state:'Alabama',lon:-88.04,lat:30.69,inGame:true,facts:['Mobile was celebrating carnival as early as 1703, long before Mardi Gras fame.','Mobile’s Cowbellion society, formed in 1830, helped start masked parades.','A World War II battleship, the USS Alabama, sits on the shore of Mobile Bay.']},
'New Orleans':{state:'Louisiana',lon:-90.07,lat:29.95,inGame:true,facts:['The “Big Easy” nickname may come from musicians who found work easily here.','The po’ boy sandwich is credited to a 1929 streetcar strike in New Orleans.','Streets that cross Canal Street in New Orleans change names on each side.']},
'Jackson':{state:'Mississippi',lon:-90.18,lat:32.30,inGame:true,facts:['Jackson sits above an extinct volcano buried about 2,900 feet down.','Before Jackson, the Choctaw town here was Chisha Foka, “among the post oaks.”','Jackson was laid out in 1822 on a grid with parks between city blocks.']},
'Memphis':{state:'Tennessee',lon:-90.05,lat:35.15,inGame:true,facts:['Memphis was named after the ancient capital of Egypt on the Nile.','Into the 1950s, Memphis was a giant market for mules.','Dyer’s Burgers here is said to reuse the same grease for over 100 years.']},
'Nashville':{state:'Tennessee',lon:-86.78,lat:36.16,inGame:true,facts:['Nashville has a full-scale replica of the Parthenon in Athens.','Goo Goo Clusters have been made in Nashville since 1912.','A radio announcer’s 1950 ad-lib helped give Nashville the name Music City.']},
'Knoxville':{state:'Tennessee',lon:-83.92,lat:35.96,inGame:true,facts:['Knoxville’s Sunsphere is a 266-foot tower topped with a gold glass ball.','The Cherokee called the Knoxville area “mulberry place.”','Sergei Rachmaninoff gave his final concert in Knoxville in 1943.']},
'Louisville':{state:'Kentucky',lon:-85.76,lat:38.25,inGame:true,facts:['Louisville is the birthplace of four heavyweight boxing champions.','At the height of disco, a Louisville company made about 90% of US mirror balls.','Old Louisville is a huge historic district made up only of Victorian homes.']},
'Cincinnati':{state:'Ohio',lon:-84.51,lat:39.10,inGame:true,facts:['The Filet-O-Fish was invented in 1962 by a McDonald’s owner near Cincinnati.','Cincinnati was once nicknamed Porkopolis for its pork business.','Cincinnati hosts baseball’s traditional opener, and kids skip school for it.']},
'Columbus':{state:'Ohio',lon:-83.00,lat:39.96,inGame:true,facts:['Chains test new products in Columbus because it is seen as a typical American city.','Columbus-style pizza is round but cut into squares.','Columbus held a 24-hour car race at the Columbus Driving Park in 1905.']},
'Cleveland':{state:'Ohio',lon:-81.69,lat:41.50,inGame:true,facts:['Superman was created in Cleveland in 1933 by Joe Shuster and Jerry Siegel.','Downtown Cleveland doubled for Manhattan in Spider-Man 3.','Jazz guitarist Django Reinhardt made his U.S. debut in Cleveland in 1946.']},
'Toledo':{state:'Ohio',lon:-83.54,lat:41.65,inGame:true,facts:['M*A*S*H’s Corporal Klinger said he was from Toledo.','The exclamation “Holy Toledo” is thought to refer to the city’s grand churches.','The Toledo Zoo is credited with the first hippoquarium-style exhibit.']},
'Detroit':{state:'Michigan',lon:-83.05,lat:42.33,inGame:true,facts:['Detroit’s name comes from the French word for “strait.”','Detroit gave the world techno music as well as Motown.','Detroit and Windsor share one of North America’s busiest border crossings.']},
'Indianapolis':{state:'Indiana',lon:-86.16,lat:39.77,inGame:true,facts:['Will Rogers called Indianapolis a farm with a monument in the middle.','Indianapolis is the Circle City because its 1821 plan centers on Monument Circle.','“Crossroads of America” first described the many railroads crossing Indianapolis.']},
'Chicago':{state:'Illinois',lon:-87.63,lat:41.88,inGame:true,facts:['Each St. Patrick’s Day, the Chicago River is dyed a bright Irish green.','In the 1850s, Chicago raised its whole city several feet to fix drainage.','The Ferris wheel debuted at Chicago’s 1893 World’s Fair.']},
'Milwaukee':{state:'Wisconsin',lon:-87.91,lat:43.04,inGame:true,facts:['Local cream-colored clay bricks gave Milwaukee the nickname Cream City.','Milwaukee’s Summerfest was once listed by Guinness as the biggest music festival.','Milwaukee once had four of the world’s largest breweries.']},
'Madison':{state:'Wisconsin',lon:-89.40,lat:43.07,inGame:true,facts:['Madison’s trademark is “Lake, City, Lake” because downtown sits between two lakes.','Madison has eight Frank Lloyd Wright buildings, more than any city outside Chicago.','Students planted 1,008 plastic flamingos on Madison’s Bascom Hill in 1979.']},
'Minneapolis':{state:'Minnesota',lon:-93.27,lat:44.98,inGame:true,facts:['The name Minneapolis joins the Dakota word for water with the Greek word for city.','In 1884 Minneapolis flour milling was worth more than anywhere else.','Prince was born in Minneapolis and lived in the area most of his life.']},
'Duluth':{state:'Minnesota',lon:-92.10,lat:46.79,inGame:true,facts:['Duluth’s port takes oceangoing ships, even though it’s far inland.','Duluth’s hills earned it the nickname San Francisco of the Midwest.','Duluth’s hilltop airport sits 820 feet higher than its lakeside one.']},
'Fargo':{state:'North Dakota',lon:-96.79,lat:46.88,inGame:true,facts:['Fargo was called the divorce capital of the Midwest in the 1880s.','The movie Fargo shows the city only briefly, in a bar scene at the start.','Fargo is named for William Fargo, who also founded Wells Fargo.']},
'Sioux Falls':{state:'South Dakota',lon:-96.73,lat:43.55,inGame:true,facts:['In the 1890s, Sioux Falls was a go-to stop for people seeking quick divorces.','The SculptureWalk in downtown Sioux Falls includes a replica of Michelangelo’s David.','Sioux Falls’ pro baseball team was once called the Fighting Pheasants.']},
'Des Moines':{state:'Iowa',lon:-93.62,lat:41.59,inGame:true,facts:['Des Moines likely comes from the French “Rivière des Moines,” River of the Monks.','Downtown Des Moines has over four miles of enclosed skywalks.','A butter cow has been a Des Moines Iowa State Fair staple since 1911.']},
'Omaha':{state:'Nebraska',lon:-95.93,lat:41.26,inGame:true,facts:['The TV dinner is counted among Omaha’s inventions.','Omaha counts the Reuben sandwich among its inventions.','Union Pacific began laying its railroad west from Omaha in 1866.']},
'Kansas City':{state:'Missouri',lon:-94.58,lat:39.10,inGame:true,facts:['Kansas City has more than 200 working fountains.','Kansas City’s many boulevards earned it the name Paris of the Plains.','Kansas City dug a streetcar tunnel under 8th Street in 1903.']},
'St Louis':{state:'Missouri',lon:-90.20,lat:38.63,inGame:true,facts:['At 630 feet, the Gateway Arch in St. Louis is the world’s tallest arch.','Forest Park in St. Louis is about 500 acres bigger than Central Park.','By the 1830s, over 150 steamboats could crowd the St. Louis levee at once.']},
'Little Rock':{state:'Arkansas',lon:-92.29,lat:34.75,inGame:true,facts:['Little Rock nearly got the name “Arkopolis” in the 1820s.','The Clinton library in Little Rock juts out over the Arkansas River.','Little Rock’s Big Dam Bridge is only for walkers and bikes, never cars or trains.']},
'Tulsa':{state:'Oklahoma',lon:-95.99,lat:36.15,inGame:true,facts:['Tulsa has one of the biggest collections of Art Deco buildings in the country.','Tulsa is nicknamed the Birthplace of Route 66.','A 76-foot Golden Driller statue guards the Tulsa County Fairgrounds.']},
'Oklahoma City':{state:'Oklahoma',lon:-97.52,lat:35.47,inGame:true,facts:['Working oil derricks stand on the grounds of the Oklahoma state capitol.','The novel Ready Player One is set in Oklahoma City in the year 2045.','Oklahoma City’s art museum holds a huge collection of Chihuly glass.']},
'Wichita':{state:'Kansas',lon:-97.34,lat:37.69,inGame:true,facts:['Pizza Hut got its start in Wichita in 1958.','The health-care brand Mentholatum was founded in Wichita in 1889.','In 1929, Wichita was dubbed the “Air Capital of the World.”']},
'Dallas':{state:'Texas',lon:-96.80,lat:32.78,inGame:true,facts:['Dallas’s White Rock Lake has a 66-acre arboretum on its shore.','Dallas’s convention center has a giant exhibit hall with no columns.','Dallas is sometimes called the heart of the Silicon Prairie.']},
'Houston':{state:'Texas',lon:-95.37,lat:29.76,inGame:true,facts:['Houston is the birthplace of the chopped and screwed hip-hop remix style.','Houston once got 20 inches of snow from a single storm in February 1895.','Houston’s livestock show and rodeo takes over the city every March.']},
'Austin':{state:'Texas',lon:-97.74,lat:30.27,inGame:true,facts:['Austin’s 165-foot moonlight towers appear in the film Dazed and Confused.','Austin’s nickname, City of the Violet Crown, comes from the glow after sunset.','Barton Springs in Austin is a huge natural swimming pool fed by springs.']},
'San Antonio':{state:'Texas',lon:-98.49,lat:29.42,inGame:true,facts:['San Antonio’s Kiddie Park has been giving kids rides since 1925.','In 1985, San Antonio moved a whole hotel three blocks, a Guinness-listed feat.','San Antonio is a UNESCO City of Creativity for Gastronomy.']},
'El Paso':{state:'Texas',lon:-106.49,lat:31.76,inGame:true,facts:['El Paso is the only major Texas city on Mountain Time.','El Paso gets about 302 sunny days a year, earning the nickname Sun City.','Franklin Mountains State Park sits entirely inside El Paso’s city limits.']},
'Amarillo':{state:'Texas',lon:-101.83,lat:35.22,inGame:true,facts:['Cadillac Ranch near Amarillo has Cadillacs buried nose-first in the ground.','Amarillo’s Big Texan gives you a free 72-ounce steak if you finish it in an hour.','Amarillo once called itself the Helium Capital of the World.']},
'Albuquerque':{state:'New Mexico',lon:-106.65,lat:35.08,inGame:true,facts:['Albuquerque’s Balloon Fiesta is the world’s largest gathering of hot-air balloons.','Albuquerque’s Sandia Peak tram is the longest aerial tramway in the Americas.','Bugs Bunny kept missing “that left turn at Albuquerque” starting in 1945.']},
'Denver':{state:'Colorado',lon:-104.99,lat:39.74,inGame:true,facts:['Denver’s mile-high mark is a benchmark on the State Capitol steps.','Denver was named to flatter a governor who had already resigned.','Denver gets around 3,100 hours of sunshine a year.']},
'Cheyenne':{state:'Wyoming',lon:-104.82,lat:41.14,inGame:true,facts:['Union Pacific tracks reached Cheyenne on November 13, 1867.','Cheyenne grew so fast it was nicknamed the “Magic City of the Plains.”','Cheyenne Frontier Days, a giant rodeo and festival, has been held since 1897.']},
'Rapid City':{state:'South Dakota',lon:-103.23,lat:44.08,inGame:true,facts:['Rapid City has life-sized bronze statues of presidents on downtown street corners.','Black Elk Peak, near Rapid City, is the highest point east of the Rockies.','In 1930, Rapid City’s Chamber of Commerce invited Al Capone to move to the area.']},
'Billings':{state:'Montana',lon:-108.50,lat:45.78,inGame:true,facts:['William Clark carved his name into Pompeys Pillar, a rock near Billings.','Billings was nicknamed the Magic City because it seemed to appear overnight.','Billings is named for Northern Pacific Railroad president Frederick Billings.']},
'Missoula':{state:'Montana',lon:-114.00,lat:46.87,inGame:true,facts:['Missoula’s university campus once lay under a glacial lake 950 feet deep.','Missoula is home to the smokejumpers’ headquarters.','Missoula’s carousel was hand-carved and built by volunteers.']},
'Salt Lake City':{state:'Utah',lon:-111.89,lat:40.76,inGame:true,facts:['Salt Lake City’s street names count blocks from the corner of Temple Square.','A homemade electric traffic light went up in Salt Lake City in 1912.','Salt Lake City’s blocks are separated by streets 132 feet wide.']},
'Boise':{state:'Idaho',lon:-116.20,lat:43.62,inGame:true,facts:['Legend says Boise got its name when someone shouted “Les bois!” at the green valley.','In the 1890s, homes along Boise’s Warm Springs Avenue were heated by hot spring water.','Boise’s downtown Basque Block honors one of the larger Basque communities in the U.S.']},
'Spokane':{state:'Washington',lon:-117.43,lat:47.66,inGame:true,facts:['Spokane hosted the 1974 World’s Fair, the first with an environmental theme.','Spokane’s Riverfront Park has a hand-carved carousel made in 1909.','The name Spokane is said to mean “children of the sun.”']},
'Seattle':{state:'Washington',lon:-122.33,lat:47.61,inGame:true,facts:['The Space Needle was built for the 1962 World’s Fair in Seattle.','Seattle’s Alki neighborhood is named for a Chinook word meaning “by and by.”','Seattle officially adopted the nickname “Emerald City” in 1982.']},
'Portland':{state:'Oregon',lon:-122.68,lat:45.52,inGame:true,facts:['Portland got its name from a coin toss, won two out of three by Pettygrove.','Portland has an extinct volcano inside its city limits.','Portland’s Rose Society was started in 1889 by Georgiana Pittock.']},
'Eugene':{state:'Oregon',lon:-123.09,lat:44.05,inGame:true,facts:['Bill Bowerman invented the waffle sole for running shoes in Eugene.','Eugene’s Saturday Market began in 1970, and vendors must make or grow what they sell.','National Lampoon’s Animal House was filmed in Eugene and nearby Cottage Grove.']},
'Bend':{state:'Oregon',lon:-121.31,lat:44.06,inGame:true,facts:['Bend is short for Farewell Bend, which was shortened because that name was taken.','Pilot Butte, an old cinder cone, sits right inside Bend’s city limits.','A lava tube system runs along the eastern edge of Bend.']},
'Reno':{state:'Nevada',lon:-119.81,lat:39.53,inGame:true,facts:['Reno’s “Biggest Little City” arch slogan came from a 1929 contest.','The Truckee River flows from Lake Tahoe through downtown Reno.','Reno once openly called itself the Divorce Capital of the World.']},
'Sacramento':{state:'California',lon:-121.49,lat:38.58,inGame:true,facts:['Downtown Sacramento was filled in with dirt, so old first floors became basements.','Sacramento’s water tower now says Farm-to-Fork as well as City of Trees.','At the railroad museum in Old Sacramento, you can ride live steam locomotives.']},
'San Francisco':{state:'California',lon:-122.42,lat:37.77,inGame:true,facts:['San Francisco has had only about 10 measurable snowfalls since 1852.','San Francisco has more than 50 hills inside the city limits.','Cable cars first carried riders up Clay Street in San Francisco in 1873.']},
'Fresno':{state:'California',lon:-119.79,lat:36.74,inGame:true,facts:['Fresno is Spanish for “ash tree,” named for ashes along the San Joaquin River.','In 1964 Fresno turned a downtown street into one of America’s first pedestrian malls.','Fresno native Audra McDonald starred in Evita and The Wiz at the Tower Theatre.']},
'Los Angeles':{state:'California',lon:-118.24,lat:34.05,inGame:true,facts:['Los Angeles began as “The Town of Our Lady the Queen of the Angels.”','The Los Angeles River was straightened and lined with 51 miles of concrete.','Los Angeles hosted the Summer Olympics in both 1932 and 1984.']},
'San Diego':{state:'California',lon:-117.16,lat:32.72,inGame:true,facts:['The San Diego Zoo grew from animals shown at a 1915 exposition here.','San Diego was once known as the tuna capital of the world.','San Diego has one of only two places on Earth where Torrey pines grow wild.']},
'Las Vegas':{state:'Nevada',lon:-115.14,lat:36.17,inGame:true,facts:['Las Vegas is Spanish for “the meadows.”','The Las Vegas Strip is actually outside the city limits.','Las Vegas was once nicknamed Atomic City for nearby nuclear tests.']},
'Flagstaff':{state:'Arizona',lon:-111.65,lat:35.20,inGame:true,facts:['Pluto was discovered in 1930 from Lowell Observatory in Flagstaff.','Flagstaff is named for a real flagpole made from a stripped pine tree.','Flagstaff is ringed by the largest unbroken ponderosa pine forest in North America.']},
'Phoenix':{state:'Arizona',lon:-112.07,lat:33.45,inGame:true,facts:['Hohokam people dug about 135 miles of irrigation canals where Phoenix now stands.','Phoenix got its name because it was a city born from an older civilization’s ruins.','Phoenix’s Musical Instrument Museum bills itself as the biggest of its kind.']},
'Tucson':{state:'Arizona',lon:-110.97,lat:32.22,inGame:true,facts:['Tucson was named a UNESCO City of Gastronomy in 2015, a first for America.','The University of Arizona in Tucson can cast giant telescope mirrors.','Tucson’s name is commonly translated as “the base of the hill is black.”']},
'Bar Harbor':{state:'Maine',lon:-68.20,lat:44.39,inGame:false,facts:['Bar Harbor is named for a sandbar that appears at low tide twice a day.','Bar Harbor was called Eden until 1918.','Cadillac Mountain, at Bar Harbor, is the highest point within 25 miles of the East Coast.']},
'Portsmouth':{state:'New Hampshire',lon:-70.76,lat:43.07,inGame:false,facts:['Portsmouth’s old name, Strawbery Banke, came from wild strawberries.','Portsmouth’s Gilley’s Diner began in 1912 as a horse-drawn lunch wagon.','The test submarine USS Albacore, with a teardrop hull, is on display in Portsmouth.']},
'Concord':{state:'New Hampshire',lon:-71.54,lat:43.21,inGame:false,facts:['Concord built the famous Concord stagecoach.','Concord got its name to mark new harmony between two towns.','Downtown Concord sidewalks were heated with steam from a nearby plant.']},
'Montpelier':{state:'Vermont',lon:-72.58,lat:44.26,inGame:false,facts:['Montpelier has long been a state capital with no McDonald’s.','Montpelier was named for the French city of Montpellier, with one letter changed.','Montpelier’s Kellogg-Hubbard Library has a copy of the Parthenon Frieze.']},
'Bennington':{state:'Vermont',lon:-73.20,lat:42.88,inGame:false,facts:['Bennington Battle Monument is a 306-foot tower, Vermont’s tallest human-made structure.','Bennington is known nationally for its pottery.','Bennington’s Silk Covered Bridge dates back to 1840.']},
'Providence':{state:'Rhode Island',lon:-71.41,lat:41.82,inGame:false,facts:['WaterFire in Providence is about 100 bonfires burning on the downtown rivers.','Providence is one of many cities claimed to be built on seven hills like Rome.','Trade magazines in the 1960s called Providence the jewelry capital of the world.']},
'Newport':{state:'Rhode Island',lon:-71.31,lat:41.49,inGame:false,facts:['Newport’s mansions were called cottages, but copied European royal palaces.','Bob Dylan’s controversial 1965 set at the Newport Folk Festival shaped folk rock.','The America’s Cup sailing races were held off Newport from 1930 to 1983.']},
'New Haven':{state:'Connecticut',lon:-72.93,lat:41.31,inGame:false,facts:['Legend says the Frisbee began as pie tins tossed on the New Haven Green.','New Haven’s Frank Pepe Pizzeria introduced its thin-crust apizza in 1925.','Some credit New Haven’s Louis’ Lunch with inventing the hamburger.']},
'Mystic':{state:'Connecticut',lon:-71.97,lat:41.35,inGame:false,facts:['More than 600 ships were built in Mystic over 135 years starting in 1784.','Mystic’s name comes from a Pequot word for a tidal river whipped into waves.','The movie Mystic Pizza took its title from a real pizza shop in Mystic.']},
'Provincetown':{state:'Massachusetts',lon:-70.19,lat:42.05,inGame:false,facts:['The Mayflower Compact was drawn up and signed in Provincetown before Plymouth.','Provincetown had a school said to be the first outdoor school for figure painting.','Provincetown became known worldwide for its artists and writers in the 1900s.']},
'Salem':{state:'Massachusetts',lon:-70.90,lat:42.52,inGame:false,facts:['America’s first living-history museum, Pioneer Village, opened in Salem in 1930.','Salem became a world-famous seaport through the China trade.','Salem’s Derby House is billed as the home of America’s first millionaire.']},
'Lake Placid':{state:'New York',lon:-73.98,lat:44.28,inGame:false,facts:['Lake Placid hosted the Winter Olympics twice, in 1932 and 1980.','The lake beside Lake Placid village is actually Mirror Lake.','Melvil Dewey, of the Dewey Decimal System, founded the Lake Placid Club.']},
'Saratoga Springs':{state:'New York',lon:-73.78,lat:43.08,inGame:false,facts:['The club sandwich is believed to have been invented at the Canfield Casino here.','Legend says potato chips were invented near here in 1854, at Moon’s Lake House.','The mineral springs here are famous because each one tastes different.']},
'Cooperstown':{state:'New York',lon:-74.92,lat:42.70,inGame:false,facts:['Legend says baseball began in a Cooperstown cow pasture, but historians reject it.','Cooperstown, home of the Baseball Hall of Fame, is a village of about 1,800 people.','Novelist James Fenimore Cooper grew up in Cooperstown, which his father founded.']},
'Niagara Falls':{state:'New York',lon:-79.05,lat:43.09,inGame:false,facts:['In 1969, engineers shut off the American Falls at Niagara for several months.','The water flow at Niagara Falls is halved at night.','Annie Edson Taylor, 63, was first over Niagara Falls in a barrel, in 1901.']},
'Rochester':{state:'New York',lon:-77.61,lat:43.16,inGame:false,facts:['Rochester ran a subway from 1927 to 1956, in the smallest city to have one.','Rochester’s French’s Mustard company once sat at an address of 1 Mustard Street.','Every December, a tower of beer kegs shaped like a Christmas tree goes up here.']},
'Atlantic City':{state:'New Jersey',lon:-74.42,lat:39.36,inGame:false,facts:['Atlantic City’s 1870 Boardwalk is described as the world’s first boardwalk.','Salt water taffy was reportedly first sold in Atlantic City in 1883.','Atlantic City’s Absecon Lighthouse is the tallest in New Jersey.']},
'Princeton':{state:'New Jersey',lon:-74.66,lat:40.35,inGame:false,facts:['Princeton’s little train shuttle is nicknamed the “Dinky.”','Albert Einstein was a fellow at Princeton’s Institute for Advanced Study.','Princeton is said to be named for Prince William of Orange.']},
'Cape May':{state:'New Jersey',lon:-74.91,lat:38.94,inGame:false,facts:['The whole city of Cape May is a National Historic Landmark.','More than 400 bird species have been recorded around Cape May.','Cape May was known as a seaside resort by the mid-1700s.']},
'Wilmington DE':{state:'Delaware',lon:-75.55,lat:39.74,inGame:false,facts:['Wilmington’s Christina River was named for a queen of Sweden.','Reggae star Bob Marley once lived in Wilmington while saving for a studio.','Swedish settlers landed at Wilmington in 1638 aboard the Kalmar Nyckel.']},
'Rehoboth Beach':{state:'Delaware',lon:-75.08,lat:38.72,inGame:false,facts:['Rehoboth Beach was once incorporated under the name Cape Henlopen City.','Rehoboth is a Hebrew word that means broad spaces.','Rehoboth Beach began in 1873 as a Methodist camp meeting retreat.']},
'Annapolis':{state:'Maryland',lon:-76.49,lat:38.98,inGame:false,facts:['Annapolis was the temporary U.S. capital after the Revolutionary War.','The Maryland State House in Annapolis has a big wooden dome built without nails.','Annapolis is often the finish line of a 3,000-mile cross-country bike race.']},
'Ocean City':{state:'Maryland',lon:-75.08,lat:38.34,inGame:false,facts:['Ocean City was once called “The Ladies’ Resort to the Ocean.”','Trimper’s Rides in Ocean City has one of the country’s oldest working carousels.','Ocean City’s inlet separated the town from Assateague Island.']},
'Hershey':{state:'Pennsylvania',lon:-76.65,lat:40.29,inGame:false,facts:['Hershey has downtown street lamps shaped like Hershey’s Kisses.','Chocolate Avenue meets Cocoa Avenue at the center of Hershey.','Wilt Chamberlain scored 100 points in a game at Hersheypark Arena in 1962.']},
'Punxsutawney':{state:'Pennsylvania',lon:-79.00,lat:40.94,inGame:false,facts:['Punxsutawney comes from a Lenape word meaning town of the sandflies.','The movie Groundhog Day was shot mostly in Woodstock, Illinois, not Punxsutawney.','Swarms of gnats plagued early Punxsutawney settlers and their livestock for years.']},
'Charleston WV':{state:'West Virginia',lon:-81.63,lat:38.35,inGame:false,facts:['Drillers searching for salt near Charleston struck a natural gas well in 1815.','Charleston’s Clay Center has a 175-seat planetarium and dome-screen cinema.','Charleston’s name was shortened in 1818 to avoid mix-ups with another Charles Town.']},
'Harpers Ferry':{state:'West Virginia',lon:-77.74,lat:39.33,inGame:false,facts:['Harpers Ferry is where the Potomac and Shenandoah rivers meet, at three state lines.','Thomas Jefferson said the view here was worth a voyage across the Atlantic.','The Appalachian Trail runs right through Harpers Ferry, its “psychological midpoint.”']},
'Wheeling':{state:'West Virginia',lon:-80.72,lat:40.06,inGame:false,facts:['Lessons from Wheeling’s 1849 suspension bridge helped build the Brooklyn Bridge.','Wheeling’s iron mills earned it the nickname Nail City.','Wheeling’s country radio show Jamboree U.S.A. ran from 1933 to 2007.']},
'Williamsburg':{state:'Virginia',lon:-76.71,lat:37.27,inGame:false,facts:['Williamsburg was once Middle Plantation, until it was renamed for King William III.','William & Mary, in Williamsburg, is America’s second-oldest college after Harvard.','Williamsburg was the capital of Virginia from 1699 to 1780.']},
'Charlottesville':{state:'Virginia',lon:-78.48,lat:38.03,inGame:false,facts:['Charlottesville is named after Queen Charlotte.','Monticello and the University of Virginia together form one UNESCO World Heritage Site.','Charlottesville’s Downtown Mall is one of the longest outdoor walking malls around.']},
'Roanoke':{state:'Virginia',lon:-79.94,lat:37.27,inGame:false,facts:['Roanoke has an 88-foot lit star perched on top of Mill Mountain.','Roanoke was once called Big Lick, after salt deposits that drew animals.','Roanoke’s transportation museum displays a US Army Jupiter rocket.']},
'Asheville':{state:'North Carolina',lon:-82.55,lat:35.60,inGame:false,facts:['Downtown Asheville has a remarkably complete collection of Art Deco buildings.','A 1928 mountain music event here is said to be the first labeled a folk festival.','The Biltmore Estate here is described as the largest privately owned house in the U.S.']},
'Wilmington NC':{state:'North Carolina',lon:-77.94,lat:34.23,inGame:false,facts:['Interstate 40 starts in Wilmington and runs about 2,550 miles to Barstow, California.','Wilmington’s film studio has a giant special-effects water tank for movie scenes.','You can tour the World War II battleship USS North Carolina in Wilmington.']},
'Kitty Hawk':{state:'North Carolina',lon:-75.71,lat:36.06,inGame:false,facts:['The Wright brothers’ flights were really about four miles away, at Kill Devil Hills.','Kitty Hawk’s name may come from an Algonquin phrase meaning a place to hunt geese.','The Apollo 14 command module was named Kitty Hawk after this town.']},
'Winston-Salem':{state:'North Carolina',lon:-80.24,lat:36.10,inGame:false,facts:['Krispy Kreme opened its very first doughnut shop in Winston-Salem in 1937.','Winston-Salem became an official port of entry despite being 200 miles inland.','Texas Pete hot sauce comes from Winston-Salem, not Texas.']},
'Myrtle Beach':{state:'South Carolina',lon:-78.89,lat:33.69,inGame:false,facts:['Myrtle Beach is named for the southern wax myrtle, a shrub that grows nearby.','The Myrtle Beach area has over 80 golf courses and over 30 mini golf courses.','Tiger Woods called Myrtle Beach the mecca of golf in 1997.']},
'Greenville':{state:'South Carolina',lon:-82.39,lat:34.85,inGame:false,facts:['Greenville was called Pleasantburg until it was renamed in 1821.','In 1994, BMW opened its first plant outside Germany near Greenville.','Greenville’s Fluor Field was designed to echo features of Fenway Park.']},
'Athens':{state:'Georgia',lon:-83.38,lat:33.96,inGame:false,facts:['According to legend, a white oak here owns itself and eight feet of land around it.','The B-52’s formed in Athens after a St. Valentine’s Day party in 1977.','The 40 Watt Club here unofficially opened on Halloween night in 1978.']},
'Macon':{state:'Georgia',lon:-83.63,lat:32.84,inGame:false,facts:['Macon is home to about 300,000 Yoshino cherry trees.','Macon’s cherry trees began with a landscaper’s mix-up in a yard in 1949.','Macon’s “Big House” was the Allman Brothers Band’s home from 1970 to 1973.']},
'Key West':{state:'Florida',lon:-81.78,lat:24.56,inGame:false,facts:['Six-toed cats at Hemingway’s Key West home descend from his own pet.','Key West’s famous southernmost point buoy is not the true southernmost point.','In 1982 Key West playfully declared independence and became the Conch Republic.']},
'St Augustine':{state:'Florida',lon:-81.31,lat:29.90,inGame:false,facts:['St. Augustine’s Hotel Ponce de Leon was powered by Edison Electric.','Flagler College in St. Augustine sits inside a former Flagler hotel.','St. Augustine’s 1565 founding was marked with a Thanksgiving Mass.']},
'Daytona Beach':{state:'Florida',lon:-81.02,lat:29.21,inGame:false,facts:['Daytona Beach lets cars drive on parts of its hard-packed sand.','Malcolm Campbell hit about 277 mph on Daytona Beach’s sand in 1935.','Daytona Beach is named for Mathias Day, who lost his land title in 1872.']},
'Pensacola':{state:'Florida',lon:-87.22,lat:30.42,inGame:false,facts:['Pensacola is called the City of Five Flags for the governments that ruled it.','Spanish settlers reached Pensacola in 1559, six years before St. Augustine.','Neil Armstrong was among the naval pilots trained at Pensacola’s air station.']},
'Huntsville':{state:'Alabama',lon:-86.59,lat:34.73,inGame:false,facts:['Huntsville’s Monte Sano mountain has a name that means “Mountain of Health.”','The cast of the film Apollo 13 trained at Space Camp in Huntsville.','A Huntsville Army team launched Explorer 1, America’s first satellite.']},
'Muscle Shoals':{state:'Alabama',lon:-87.67,lat:34.74,inGame:false,facts:['Aretha Franklin, Cher and the Rolling Stones all recorded in Muscle Shoals.','Nobody is sure how Muscle Shoals got its name, but mussel shells are one theory.','Congress turned down Henry Ford’s $5 million offer for Wilson Dam near Muscle Shoals.']},
'Montgomery':{state:'Alabama',lon:-86.30,lat:32.37,inGame:false,facts:['Montgomery ran electric streetcars across the whole city starting in 1886.','Montgomery’s Hank Williams Museum holds a huge collection of his memorabilia.','Writer Zelda Sayre, later Zelda Fitzgerald, was born in Montgomery.']},
'Biloxi':{state:'Mississippi',lon:-88.89,lat:30.40,inGame:false,facts:['Biloxi’s lighthouse was built in Baltimore, then shipped south.','Blessing fishing boats in the U.S. seems to have begun in Biloxi in 1929.','The Gulf Coast around Biloxi was once called the Poor Man’s Riviera.']},
'Oxford':{state:'Mississippi',lon:-89.52,lat:34.37,inGame:false,facts:['Oxford was named after Oxford, England, to sound like a center of learning.','When Oxford’s banks closed in the Depression, Neilson’s store cashed paychecks.','The Ole Miss Power House in Oxford is a setting Faulkner used in a 1930 novel.']},
'Tupelo':{state:'Mississippi',lon:-88.70,lat:34.26,inGame:false,facts:['Tupelo was the first city to get power from the TVA, in 1934.','Tupelo was first called Gum Pond, reportedly for its blackgum trees.','Tupelo is known around the world as the birthplace of Elvis Presley.']},
'Vicksburg':{state:'Mississippi',lon:-90.88,lat:32.35,inGame:false,facts:['Coca-Cola was first bottled in Vicksburg, by candy maker Joseph Biedenharn.','A canal finished in 1903 brought the Mississippi River back to Vicksburg.','Vicksburg’s Theatre Guild has staged Gold in the Hills since 1936.']},
'Baton Rouge':{state:'Louisiana',lon:-91.19,lat:30.45,inGame:false,facts:['Baton Rouge is French for “red stick,” a pole that marked a tribal boundary.','Baton Rouge is the farthest upstream Mississippi port that Panamax ships can reach.','Baton Rouge’s 450-foot capitol was the South’s tallest building when finished.']},
'Lafayette':{state:'Louisiana',lon:-92.02,lat:30.22,inGame:false,facts:['Lafayette was called Vermilionville until it was renamed in 1884.','In 2014, Lafayette was named the “Happiest City in America.”','Zydeco star Buckwheat Zydeco came from Lafayette.']},
'Shreveport':{state:'Louisiana',lon:-93.75,lat:32.53,inGame:false,facts:['Elvis Presley sang on the Louisiana Hayride radio show in Shreveport in 1954.','Shreveport has held a crawfish festival called Mudbug Madness every May since 1984.','The 2007 film The Great Debaters, with Denzel Washington, was shot in Shreveport.']},
'Hot Springs':{state:'Arkansas',lon:-93.06,lat:34.50,inGame:false,facts:['Water now bubbling up in Hot Springs fell as rain about 4,400 years ago.','About a million gallons of 143 degree water flow from Hot Springs’ springs each day.','Racing season at Hot Springs’ Oaklawn track is nicknamed the “Fifth Season.”']},
'Bentonville':{state:'Arkansas',lon:-94.21,lat:36.37,inGame:false,facts:['Sam Walton opened his Walton’s 5 and 10 on Bentonville’s square in 1951.','Alice Walton founded Bentonville’s Crystal Bridges art museum, which opened in 2011.','Bentonville is on the Razorback Greenway, a 36-mile trail across the region.']},
'Eureka Springs':{state:'Arkansas',lon:-93.74,lat:36.40,inGame:false,facts:['Eureka Springs has more than 140 springs, and every one of them is cold water.','Streets wind around Eureka Springs, and few of them meet at right angles.','Architect E. Fay Jones designed the famous Thorncrown Chapel near Eureka Springs.']},
'Chattanooga':{state:'Tennessee',lon:-85.31,lat:35.05,inGame:false,facts:['Chattanooga’s Gig City nickname comes from its super-fast internet.','Chattanooga is called the birthplace of the tow truck.','Chattanooga made its own city typeface in 2012.']},
'Gatlinburg':{state:'Tennessee',lon:-83.51,lat:35.71,inGame:false,facts:['Gatlinburg uses numbered intersections in the heart of town.','A week-long bridge tournament here draws more than 3,000 card players.','The Gatlinburg SkyBridge is a footbridge that spans two mountains.']},
'Lexington':{state:'Kentucky',lon:-84.50,lat:38.04,inGame:false,facts:['A Jif plant in Lexington makes more peanut butter than any other factory on Earth.','In 1958, Lexington put in place a first-in-the-nation urban growth boundary.','Keeneland, in Lexington, is home to the world’s largest Thoroughbred auction house.']},
'Bowling Green':{state:'Kentucky',lon:-86.44,lat:36.99,inGame:false,facts:['Every Corvette built since 1981 has come from the Bowling Green plant.','Bowling Green began as “Bolin Green” in 1798, maybe named for a New York park.','Bowling Green is home to one of the larger Bosnian communities in the country.']},
'Sandusky':{state:'Ohio',lon:-82.71,lat:41.45,inGame:false,facts:['Callahan Auto Parts from the movie Tommy Boy is set in Sandusky.','Cedar Point, in Sandusky, is nicknamed America’s Roller Coast.','Since 2008, boaters from as far as Toledo and Cleveland have thrown a barge party here.']},
'Dayton':{state:'Ohio',lon:-84.19,lat:39.76,inGame:false,facts:['In 1890, Dayton granted more patents per person than any other U.S. city.','Dayton engineer Charles Kettering helped build the first electric cash register.','Mike-sell’s of Dayton is called the oldest potato chip company in the U.S.']},
'South Bend':{state:'Indiana',lon:-86.25,lat:41.68,inGame:false,facts:['South Bend is named for the river’s southernmost bend nearby.','Studebaker grew from a wagon shop in South Bend into a car maker.','The South Bend Blue Sox played every season of the All-American Girls baseball league.']},
'Santa Claus':{state:'Indiana',lon:-86.91,lat:38.12,inGame:false,facts:['Santa Claus began as Santa Fe, but that name was taken for a post office.','Volunteer elves at the post office here answer children’s letters to Santa.','Santa Claus Land opened here in 1946 and claims to be the first theme park.']},
'Mackinac Island':{state:'Michigan',lon:-84.62,lat:45.85,inGame:false,facts:['Cars have been banned on Mackinac Island since 1898.','A state highway circles Mackinac Island with no motor vehicles allowed.','More than 80 percent of Mackinac Island is a state park.']},
'Grand Rapids':{state:'Michigan',lon:-85.67,lat:42.96,inGame:false,facts:['In 1945, Grand Rapids became the first US city to add fluoride to its water.','ArtPrize, an art contest decided by public vote, began in Grand Rapids in 2009.','La Grande Vitesse, a Calder sculpture in Grand Rapids, means “the great swiftness.”']},
'Battle Creek':{state:'Michigan',lon:-85.18,lat:42.32,inGame:false,facts:['Reformer Sojourner Truth made Battle Creek her chosen home.','Corn flakes began in Battle Creek when liquid cornmeal spilled on a hot surface.','C. W. Post invented Grape-Nuts in Battle Creek after being inspired by Kellogg.']},
'Springfield':{state:'Illinois',lon:-89.65,lat:39.78,inGame:false,facts:['Abraham Lincoln’s home in Springfield was the only house he ever owned.','The horseshoe sandwich, an open-faced pile with fries and cheese sauce, began here.','Springfield spells it “chilli” and is the Chilli Capital of the Civilized World.']},
'Galena':{state:'Illinois',lon:-90.43,lat:42.42,inGame:false,facts:['Galena is named for lead ore, which drew a mining rush in the 1820s.','Ulysses S. Grant and eight other Civil War generals lived in Galena.','About 85 percent of Galena’s buildings sit inside its historic district.']},
'Metropolis':{state:'Illinois',lon:-88.73,lat:37.15,inGame:false,facts:['Metropolis has a bronze Superman statue with a lucky spot on its boot to rub.','About 30,000 fans arrive each summer for the Metropolis Superman Celebration.','DC Comics and the Illinois legislature both approved Metropolis as Superman’s hometown.']},
'Green Bay':{state:'Wisconsin',lon:-88.02,lat:44.51,inGame:false,facts:['The Green Bay Packers are owned by a nonprofit made up of their fans.','Jean Nicolet named this spot “La Baye Verte” for the greenish water.','A fur-trading post was already operating at Green Bay by 1655.']},
'Wisconsin Dells':{state:'Wisconsin',lon:-89.77,lat:43.63,inGame:false,facts:['Amphibious “duck” tours have run in the Dells since 1946.','“Dells” comes from French “dalles,” meaning rapids or narrows.','Wisconsin Dells was called Kilbourn until it took its new name in 1931.']},
'Bemidji':{state:'Minnesota',lon:-94.88,lat:47.47,inGame:false,facts:['Bemidji’s Ojibwe name means a lake with crossing waters.','The Mississippi River flows right through Lake Bemidji.','A fireplace in Bemidji’s visitor center uses 900 stones from every state.']},
'Dyersville':{state:'Iowa',lon:-91.12,lat:42.48,inGame:false,facts:['The 1989 film Field of Dreams was shot on a farm near Dyersville.','Dyersville calls itself the Farm Toy Capital of the World.','Dyersville’s basilica has twin steeples that stand 212 feet tall.']},
'Davenport':{state:'Iowa',lon:-90.58,lat:41.52,inGame:false,facts:['Near Davenport, the Mississippi River flows from east to west.','Otto Rohwedder, inventor of mass-produced sliced bread, was a Davenport native.','Davenport native Bix Beiderbecke recorded “Davenport Blues” in 1925.']},
'Branson':{state:'Missouri',lon:-93.22,lat:36.64,inGame:false,facts:['Branson’s Titanic Museum features a half-scale replica of the ship and iceberg.','Branson is named for Reuben Branson, a storekeeper and postmaster.','The Shepherd of the Hills, a 1907 novel set near Branson, made its author a millionaire.']},
'Hannibal':{state:'Missouri',lon:-91.36,lat:39.71,inGame:false,facts:['Hannibal has an inland lighthouse for Mark Twain, with 244 steps.','Hannibal’s Tom Sawyer Days include a fence painting contest.','Cement for the Empire State Building came from Ilasco, next to Hannibal.']},
'St Joseph':{state:'Missouri',lon:-94.85,lat:39.77,inGame:false,facts:['St. Joseph was one end of the Pony Express route.','Jesse James lived in St. Joseph under the name Mr. Howard.','St. Joseph’s Pony Express Museum sits in the old company stables.']},
'Dodge City':{state:'Kansas',lon:-100.02,lat:37.76,inGame:false,facts:['Harley-Davidson’s “hog” mascot began with a piglet at a race in Dodge City.','Dodge City renamed Walnut Street as Gunsmoke Street in 1959 to honor the TV show.','The phrase “get out of Dodge” was made popular by the TV show Gunsmoke.']},
'Abilene':{state:'Kansas',lon:-97.21,lat:38.92,inGame:false,facts:['Abilene started as a stagecoach stop called Mud Creek.','Abilene’s founders reportedly opened a Bible and used the first place name they found.','Sprint’s roots trace back to a telephone company in Abilene.']},
'Lincoln':{state:'Nebraska',lon:-96.70,lat:40.81,inGame:false,facts:['Lincoln began as a village called Lancaster before being renamed.','Lincoln was founded on salt flats and marshes that drew early settlers.','The Runza restaurant chain began in Lincoln.']},
'Alliance':{state:'Nebraska',lon:-102.87,lat:42.10,inGame:false,facts:['Carhenge, a Stonehenge copy near Alliance, is built from 38 vehicles.','Alliance was first called Grand Lake until the railroad renamed it.','A family built Carhenge on a farm just north of Alliance in 1987.']},
'Medora':{state:'North Dakota',lon:-103.52,lat:46.91,inGame:false,facts:['The Marquis de Mores named Medora after his wife, Medora von Hoffman.','The Marquis built a meatpacking plant here to ship refrigerated beef to Chicago.','A Medora hotel renamed itself the Rough Riders Hotel after Roosevelt’s 1903 visit.']},
'Bismarck':{state:'North Dakota',lon:-100.78,lat:46.81,inGame:false,facts:['Before Bismarck got its name in 1873, the town was called Edwinton.','In 2007, about 9,000 people made snow angels at the capitol here.','Bismarck’s capitol is a 19-story Art Deco tower finished in 1934.']},
'Deadwood':{state:'South Dakota',lon:-103.73,lat:44.38,inGame:false,facts:['Deadwood was named by settlers for the dead trees in its gulch.','Deadwood is a National Historic Landmark for its frontier architecture.','Kevin Costner once owned a Deadwood casino called the Midnight Star.']},
'Wall':{state:'South Dakota',lon:-102.24,lat:43.99,inGame:false,facts:['Wall is named for a natural wall of rock near the Badlands.','Dorothy Hustead’s 1936 idea for free ice water signs put Wall Drug on the map.','Wall Drug’s early signs were modeled on Burma Shave highway signs.']},
'Mitchell':{state:'South Dakota',lon:-98.03,lat:43.71,inGame:false,facts:['Mitchell’s Corn Palace also doubles as a basketball arena.','The Corn Palace murals in Mitchell get a new theme every fall at harvest.','Mitchell has an excavated Native American village protected under an Archeodome.']},
'Galveston':{state:'Texas',lon:-94.80,lat:29.30,inGame:false,facts:['Galveston is said to grow over 100 kinds of oleander.','The Strand in Galveston was once called the Wall Street of the South.','Galveston hosts a Victorian Christmas festival called Dickens on the Strand.']},
'Marfa':{state:'Texas',lon:-104.02,lat:30.31,inGame:false,facts:['Marfa was named for a character in a Jules Verne novel.','The first written record of the Marfa lights dates to 1883.','The 1956 film Giant was filmed in and around Marfa.']},
'Fredericksburg':{state:'Texas',lon:-98.87,lat:30.28,inGame:false,facts:['Fredericksburg is known as the Peach Capital of Texas.','Old-time German residents called Fredericksburg “Fritztown.”','An old railroad tunnel near Fredericksburg shelters over 3 million bats.']},
'Roswell':{state:'New Mexico',lon:-104.52,lat:33.39,inGame:false,facts:['Legend says a UFO crashed near Roswell in 1947, and now it has a yearly UFO Festival.','Rocket pioneer Robert Goddard did much of his early work in Roswell.','Roswell is home to one of the world’s largest mozzarella factories.']},
'Santa Fe':{state:'New Mexico',lon:-105.94,lat:35.69,inGame:false,facts:['At 6,998 feet, Santa Fe is the highest US state capital.','Santa Fe’s full name was La Villa Real de la Santa Fe de San Francisco de Asís.','Santa Fe rules require a Spanish Territorial or Pueblo look for many buildings.']},
'Truth or Consequences':{state:'New Mexico',lon:-107.25,lat:33.13,inGame:false,facts:['In 1950 this town renamed itself after the radio show Truth or Consequences.','Truth or Consequences was called Hot Springs until 1950.','By the late 1930s, T or C had one hot springs spa for every 75 residents.']},
'Colorado Springs':{state:'Colorado',lon:-104.82,lat:38.83,inGame:false,facts:['Nikola Tesla picked Colorado Springs for his lab, drawn by its lightning.','English settlers gave Colorado Springs the nickname Little London.','Colorado Springs is nicknamed Olympic City USA and hosts a US Olympic training center.']},
'Aspen':{state:'Colorado',lon:-106.82,lat:39.19,inGame:false,facts:['Aspen was first called Ute City before it was named for its aspen trees.','In 1891 and 1892, Aspen out-mined Leadville as a silver district.','Aspen’s Buttermilk ski area became home of the Winter X Games in 2002.']},
'Durango':{state:'Colorado',lon:-107.88,lat:37.28,inGame:false,facts:['Durango is named after a Mexican city that a former governor liked.','Durango was laid out by a railroad that wanted to reach Silverton.','A 1950 film with young Marilyn Monroe was shot where Fort Lewis College now stands.']},
'Jackson WY':{state:'Wyoming',lon:-110.76,lat:43.48,inGame:false,facts:['Jackson’s town square is known for its arches built from elk antlers.','Each spring, Boy Scouts sell elk antlers gathered at the National Elk Refuge in Jackson.','Jackson elected an all-woman town government in 1920.']},
'Cody':{state:'Wyoming',lon:-109.06,lat:44.53,inGame:false,facts:['Buffalo Bill built Cody’s Irma Hotel in 1902 and named it for his daughter.','Cody’s Buffalo Bill Center of the West brings five museums under one roof.','Cody is the closest town to Yellowstone’s east entrance.']},
'Bozeman':{state:'Montana',lon:-111.04,lat:45.68,inGame:false,facts:['Bozeman once grew about 75 percent of the nation’s seed peas.','In Star Trek: First Contact, Bozeman is where the first warp ship launches.','Bozeman’s Museum of the Rockies is a Smithsonian affiliate.']},
'Butte':{state:'Montana',lon:-112.53,lat:46.00,inGame:false,facts:['Butte’s Our Lady of the Rockies statue was airlifted to the Continental Divide.','Butte has held a St. Patrick’s Day celebration since 1882.','Cornish pasties became a Butte staple as easy meals for miners.']},
'Coeur d’Alene':{state:'Idaho',lon:-116.78,lat:47.68,inGame:false,facts:['The name Coeur d’Alene is French for “heart of an awl.”','A resort golf hole here has a green that floats out on the lake.','Bald eagles gather at Lake Coeur d’Alene each winter to feed on kokanee.']},
'Twin Falls':{state:'Idaho',lon:-114.46,lat:42.56,inGame:false,facts:['You can BASE jump off the Perrine Bridge here year-round without a permit.','Evel Knievel tried to jump the Snake River Canyon near here in 1974.','The Perrine Bridge here stands about 486 feet above the Snake River.']},
'Moab':{state:'Utah',lon:-109.55,lat:38.57,inGame:false,facts:['In 1890, some locals petitioned to rename Moab “Vina.”','Moab’s population grew nearly 500% in a few years of uranium boom.','Moab’s Slickrock Trail helped make it a mountain biking hotspot.']},
'St George':{state:'Utah',lon:-113.58,lat:37.10,inGame:false,facts:['St. George hosted the Ironman World Championship in 2021, a first outside Hawaii.','Cafe Rio started in St. George in 1997.','St. George’s Dixie nickname comes from a cotton-growing mission.']},
'Carson City':{state:'Nevada',lon:-119.77,lat:39.16,inGame:false,facts:['Carson City once had its own U.S. Mint branch, making coins from 1870 to 1893.','Part of Carson City’s limits reach into the middle of Lake Tahoe.','Ferris wheel inventor George Ferris once lived in Carson City.']},
'Tonopah':{state:'Nevada',lon:-117.23,lat:38.07,inGame:false,facts:['Tonopah’s name is said to mean “hidden spring.”','The Mizpah Hotel in Tonopah was once Nevada’s tallest building.','Tonopah’s nearby test range helped develop the F-117 Nighthawk jet.']},
'Sedona':{state:'Arizona',lon:-111.76,lat:34.87,inGame:false,facts:['Sedona’s McDonald’s has blue arches instead of golden ones.','Sedona’s red rocks have starred in Hollywood films since at least 1923.','Sedona was named for Sedona Schnebly, whose husband was the first postmaster.']},
'Tombstone':{state:'Arizona',lon:-110.07,lat:31.71,inGame:false,facts:['Tombstone’s mines produced about 32 million troy ounces of silver.','The Bird Cage Theatre in Tombstone stayed open around the clock until 1889.','Guinness once named a rosebush planted in Tombstone in 1885 the world’s largest.']},
'Winslow':{state:'Arizona',lon:-110.70,lat:35.02,inGame:false,facts:['Winslow got its fame from the Eagles’ 1972 song “Take It Easy.”','Winslow’s La Posada hotel was designed by Mary Colter and opened in 1930.','Charles Lindbergh designed the airport in Winslow.']},
'Monterey':{state:'California',lon:-121.89,lat:36.60,inGame:false,facts:['Monterey became the capital of the Californias back in 1777.','Cannery Row in Monterey was renamed in 1953 to honor writer John Steinbeck.','Monterey’s post office, opened in 1849, is said to be the first west of the Rockies.']},
'Palm Springs':{state:'California',lon:-116.55,lat:33.83,inGame:false,facts:['A tram near Palm Springs climbs two and a half miles up to 8,516 feet.','Spanish explorers may have named Palm Springs for “The Palm of God’s hand.”','Sonny Bono was once the mayor of Palm Springs.']},
'Barstow':{state:'California',lon:-117.02,lat:34.90,inGame:false,facts:['Barstow is named for a railroad president, William Barstow Strong.','The McDonald’s at Barstow Station is built from three railroad cars.','Barstow’s 1911 Harvey House hotel and station now holds a Route 66 museum.']},
'Astoria':{state:'Oregon',lon:-123.83,lat:46.19,inGame:false,facts:['The bridge from Astoria to Washington stretches 4.1 miles across the Columbia River.','The 1985 movie The Goonies was filmed on location right here in Astoria.','The 125-foot Astoria Column was partly funded by John Jacob Astor’s great-grandson.']},
'Leavenworth':{state:'Washington',lon:-120.66,lat:47.60,inGame:false,facts:['The Edelweiss Hotel was the first building in Leavenworth to go Bavarian, in 1965.','Leavenworth’s Nutcracker Museum holds more than 7,000 nutcrackers.','Leavenworth’s Bavarian look began as a plan to revive a struggling logging town.']}
};
